textosLlegan("theory", {"hf-kant-juicios":{"html":"<p>In Topic 19 we saw that, for Kant, knowing is not copying the world: the subject contributes certain a priori forms, space and time in sensibility and the <strong>categories</strong> in the understanding. But where does Kant get the idea that there are exactly twelve categories, and precisely these ones? This exploration follows his path step by step, starting where he started: <strong>Aristotle's logic</strong>, with a stop at <strong>Leibniz</strong>. From there it moves from the table of <strong>judgements</strong> to the table of <strong>categories</strong>, from the categories to the <strong>schemata</strong> and to the <strong>principles</strong> that govern all experience, and from there to the <strong>Ideas</strong> with which reason tries to go further. Along the way we will correct three examples that are repeated a lot and are wrong.</p>\n\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: ‘If I press the switch, the light comes on’. Have you ever seen the causal relation between the two things, or have you only seen one thing and then another? Hume said the latter. Kant will answer that causality is not seen, but that it is not a mere habit either.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Kant and Aristotle's logic</h2>\n<p>Kant admired logic. In the preface to the second edition of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1787) he holds it up as an example of a body of knowledge that has found the ‘secure path of a science’:</p>\n<blockquote><p>‘That logic has followed this secure path since the earliest times can be seen from the fact that, since Aristotle, it has not had to take a single step backwards […]. What is remarkable is that until now it has not been able to take a single step forwards either, so that, to all appearances, it seems closed and complete.’ — Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, preface to the 2nd edition, B VIII (own translation)</p></blockquote>\n<p>For Kant, logic advances securely because it deals only with the <strong>formal rules of all thought</strong>, without looking at the objects they are applied to (B IX). His dream is for metaphysics to find an equally firm path. That is why, when he looks for the forms with which the understanding unites what we perceive, he does not invent them: he takes them from that logic which he believed to be finished.</p>\n<p>What was that logic? The essentials came from Aristotle (4th century BC), in the books that tradition gathered under the name <em>Organon</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terms and propositions.</strong> The simplest proposition joins a subject and a predicate: ‘S is P’. It can speak about all or about some (quantity) and it can affirm or deny (quality): these are the four propositions that the Middle Ages called A, E, I and O (<em>On Interpretation</em>, 7).</li>\n<li><strong>The syllogism.</strong> Two premises that share a middle term and a conclusion that follows necessarily: ‘every animal is mortal; every human being is an animal; therefore every human being is mortal’ (<em>Prior Analytics</em>, I 4-7).</li>\n<li><strong>Modality.</strong> Aristotle also studied the propositions ‘it is possible that…’ and ‘it is necessary that…’ (<em>On Interpretation</em>, 12-13) and the syllogisms that combine them.</li>\n<li><strong>Indefinite names.</strong> This is what he called expressions such as ‘non-man’, which do not name anything specific (<em>On Interpretation</em>, 2). This is the origin of Kant's <strong>infinite</strong> judgement (‘the soul is non-mortal’).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not everything Kant calls logic comes from Aristotle. Reasoning with ‘if…, then’ and with ‘either… or’ was developed later by his pupil Theophrastus and, above all, by the Stoics, such as Chrysippus. It reached the Middle Ages through Boethius (6th century) and was gradually added to the textbooks. Kant taught his logic classes with one of them, the <em>Excerpt from the Doctrine of Reason</em> by Georg Friedrich Meier (1752).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Heading in Kant's table</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Quantity</strong>: universal, particular, singular</td><td>Universal and particular, from Aristotle. Tradition treated the singular judgement (‘Socrates is mortal’) as a case of the universal; Kant separates it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Quality</strong>: affirmative, negative, infinite</td><td>Affirmation and negation, from Aristotle; the infinite, from his ‘indefinite name’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Relation</strong>: categorical, hypothetical, disjunctive</td><td>The categorical (‘S is P’) is Aristotle's. The hypothetical and the disjunctive come from the Stoics and from later textbooks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Modality</strong>: problematic, assertoric, apodictic</td><td>The possible and the necessary, from Aristotle. Kant turns it into a separate heading, one that does not change the content of the judgement.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘If Kant believed logic was finished, he copied it as it was’. No: he rearranged it into four headings with three moments each and added forms that general logic considered unnecessary, because he was not interested only in the correctness of reasoning, but in how the experience of objects is built.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Between Aristotle and Kant: Leibniz's logic</h2>\n<p>Between Aristotle and Kant there was a giant of logic whom Kant was hardly able to read: <strong>Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</strong> (1646-1716). Between 1670 and 1690 he filled hundreds of drafts with ideas on four subjects: the syllogism, a ‘universal calculus’ of thought, the logic of propositions and the logic of the possible and the necessary. Almost none of it was published in his lifetime. The papers stayed in the royal library in Hanover until the Frenchman Louis Couturat edited them in 1903, when modern logic was already under way. That is why they influenced neither Kant nor Boole, who learnt of them more than a year after publishing <em>The Laws of Thought</em> (1854). The <a href=\"#leibniz\">Leibniz’s Workshop</a> tells the story of his life, his calculating machine and his dream of ‘let us calculate!’; here we focus on his logic.</p>\n<p>What follows summarises the state of the question according to Wolfgang Lenzen, one of the leading specialists, in the <em>Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy</em>. His starting point is a history of research with a twist in it. Couturat, and later William and Martha Kneale (1962), judged Leibniz's logic to be a failure, and other authors believed that his way of understanding concepts led to contradictions. Since the work of Albert Dürr (1930), Nicholas Rescher (1954) and Raili Kauppi (1960), and later of Hans Burkhardt, Massimo Mugnai and Lenzen himself, what was in those drafts has gradually been reconstructed. Today <strong>four calculi</strong> are recognised in them, several of them a century and a half ahead of modern logic.</p>\n\n<h3>The syllogism, put in order</h3>\n<p>Leibniz started from the school logic of his time, whose standard textbook was the <em>Port-Royal Logic</em> (Arnauld and Nicole). He added three novelties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Axioms.</strong> Around 1682 he showed that all the valid moods can be derived from very few principles. From the classical principle that what is said of all is said of each one (<em>dictum de omni et nullo</em>) come the four perfect moods of the first figure: Barbara, Celarent, Darii and Ferio. With them and with a trivial truth, ‘some B is B’, he proves subalternation. With a rule of going backwards (<em>regressus</em>) he obtains the moods of the second and third figures, and with the laws of conversion, those of the fourth.</li>\n<li><strong>Characteristic numbers</strong> (1679). In the simple version, each concept is a number and ‘every S is P’ is true if the number of P divides the number of S: if ‘animal’ is 2 and ‘rational’ is 3, ‘human being’ is 6. To handle negative and particular propositions as well, Leibniz ended up giving each concept a <strong>pair</strong> of numbers, one positive and one negative. In 1951 Jan Łukasiewicz proved that this system yields exactly the valid syllogisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagrams.</strong> In a draft written after 1686, <em>On the Testing of Logical Form by Drawing Lines</em>, he draws each proposition with circles (the ones that would later be called Euler circles) and with horizontal segments. In this way he checks, for example, that Ferio is valid and that a mood of the fourth figure with premises A and O is not. Later he refined the lines, marking with vertical strokes how far each term extends. Euler rediscovered the circles in 1768 without knowing Leibniz's work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is how Leibniz drew the four propositions with lines. Each class is a segment, and the dotted stretches are the parts about which we do not know whether they contain anything or not:</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:120-230|P:50-330\"><figcaption><strong>A</strong> · All S are P: the S segment lies inside the P segment.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-160|P:220-330\"><figcaption><strong>E</strong> · No S are P: the two segments do not touch.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-230:50-160|P:160-330:230-330\"><figcaption><strong>I</strong> · Some S are P: they share a stretch; the dotted part may or may not exist.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-230:160-230|P:160-330\"><figcaption><strong>O</strong> · Some S are not P: a stretch of S lies outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>Leibniz also faced the problem of <strong>existential import</strong>, the problem of the unicorns from Topic 4 in Year 1. His way out was original: the domain of logic is not the things that exist, but <strong>possible individuals</strong>, ‘the region of ideas’. ‘Every unicorn has a horn’ speaks about possible unicorns; it is enough that the concept of a unicorn is not contradictory. In this way all the relations of the square of opposition are preserved without any commitment to what there is in the world.</p>\n\n<h3>An algebra of concepts</h3>\n<p>The core of his ‘universal calculus’ (around 1679-1690), above all in the <em>General Inquiries about the Analysis of Notions and Truths</em> (1686), is an algebra with four pieces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Containing.</strong> ‘Every A is B’ is reduced to ‘A contains B’. Leibniz defines it like this: A contains B when A is the same as ‘A and B’. ‘Human being’ is the same as ‘human being and animal’.</li>\n<li><strong>Joining.</strong> AB is the concept that brings A and B together: ‘rational animal’.</li>\n<li><strong>Negating.</strong> Non-A is the opposite concept.</li>\n<li><strong>Being possible.</strong> A concept is possible if it does not contain a contradiction. ‘A and non-A’ is the impossible concept par excellence, and Leibniz accepts that it <strong>contains all the others</strong>: from a contradiction anything follows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With this last law, the system turns out to be <strong>equivalent to Boolean algebra</strong>, the algebra of classes that we saw in Year 1, a hundred and sixty years before Boole. That is Lenzen's thesis.</p>\n<p>There is a difference of approach. Leibniz thinks of concepts in terms of their <strong>content</strong> (intension), whereas Boole and present-day logic think of them in terms of their <strong>extension</strong>, that is, the things they apply to. Leibniz already saw that the two readings run in opposite directions and are equivalent. ‘Human being’ <em>contains</em> ‘animal’, whereas the class of human beings <em>is inside</em> the class of animals (<em>New Essays</em>, IV, 17, § 8).</p>\n\n<h3>Quantifying over concepts and the gold mistake</h3>\n<p>Leibniz went one step further: using letters for ‘indefinite’ concepts, which work like the quantifiers of present-day logic. ‘Every A is B’ is equivalent to ‘there is a concept Y such that A is the same as BY’: ‘human being’ is ‘animal’ plus something, ‘rational’. With them he also defines the <strong>complete individual concept</strong>, the one that includes everything that will happen to an individual, a central idea of his metaphysics.</p>\n<p>In 1679, with an example about gold, Leibniz applied a rule too generally. He ended up confusing ‘A <strong>is not</strong> B’ with ‘A <strong>is non</strong>-B’ and later realised his mistake. It is exactly the distinction that Kant will turn into a heading of his table: the negative judgement as opposed to the <strong>infinite</strong> one (section 5).</p>\n\n<h3>Adding and subtracting concepts</h3>\n<p>Around 1686-1687 Leibniz also tried out a calculus of ‘plus’ and ‘minus’: joining collections and taking one away from another. There are some curious laws, such as A + A = A: joining something with itself adds nothing. He left it unfinished and with contradictions, because he tried to copy arithmetic too closely and admitted things ‘less than nothing’. Specialists have repaired it, and it contains, for example, a way of obtaining what two collections have in common by means of additions and subtractions.</p>\n\n<h3>Propositions as concepts</h3>\n<p>Between 1683 and 1686 Leibniz realised that his algebra of concepts also worked for propositions. You only have to read ‘A contains B’ as ‘if α, then β’; ‘A and B’ as ‘α and β’; ‘non-A’ as ‘not α’; and ‘A is possible’ as ‘it is possible that α’. He took the opposite route to Boole, who started from classes.</p>\n<p>And there is more. The conditional that results is not the truth-table one, but a stronger one: ‘if α, then β’ means that it is <strong>not possible</strong> for α to be true and β false. It is the <strong>strict implication</strong> that C. I. Lewis would present as a novelty in the 20th century, and the one that appears in section 6 as the closest to Kant's hypothetical judgement. Leibniz had described it almost two and a half centuries earlier.</p>\n\n<h3>The possible, the necessary and the obligatory</h3>\n<p>Leibniz defined the necessary, the possible and the impossible in terms of ‘possible cases’, and this is the origin of the idea of <strong>possible worlds</strong> made famous by the <em>Theodicy</em> (1710), with its ‘best of all possible worlds’, which Voltaire mocked in <em>Candide</em>. Its laws are those of present-day modal logic: the necessary is what cannot possibly not be, and the necessary is possible. But its semantics is simpler, because it does not distinguish which worlds are ‘accessible’ from others. Moreover, he calls <strong>contingent</strong> whatever is not necessary, and not, as today, whatever is neither necessary nor impossible.</p>\n<p>As a jurist, he carried these same relations over to morality. He used the <strong>good man</strong> (<em>vir bonus</em>) as the measure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>obligatory</strong> is what it is necessary for the good man to do;</li>\n<li><strong>permitted</strong>, what it is possible for him to do;</li>\n<li><strong>forbidden</strong>, what it is impossible for him to do.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is a <strong>deontic logic</strong>, the logic of what ought to be. It was already emerging in his law thesis on conditions (1665) and in the <em>Elements of Natural Law</em> (1669-1670). Reducing what ought to be to what is necessary in this way is attributed to the logician Alan Ross Anderson (1958), almost three hundred years later.</p>\n\n<h3>The laws of identity: ‘Leibniz's law’ and indiscernibles</h3>\n<p>Leibniz places two great principles at the basis of all reasoning, the principle of contradiction and the principle of sufficient reason, which have their own history (section 3). Here we focus on two other laws, about <strong>identity</strong>, which bear his name and which should not be confused, because one is the converse of the other:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>What it says</th><th>In present-day logic</th><th>Is it accepted today?</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Indiscernibility of identicals</strong> (‘Leibniz's law’)</td><td>If a and b are the same thing, whatever holds of one holds of the other. Leibniz formulated it as a definition: terms are the same if one can be substituted for the other without changing the truth (<em>salva veritate</em>), like ‘triangle’ and ‘trilateral’ (<em>Specimen calculi universalis</em>).</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">a = b → (Fa → Fb)</span></td><td>Yes: it is a law of the logic of identity. Frege took the idea of substitution from Leibniz.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Identity of indiscernibles</strong></td><td>If whatever holds of a holds of b, they are the same thing: there are not in nature two beings exactly alike that differ only in being two (<em>Discourse on Metaphysics</em>, § 9; <em>Monadology</em>, § 9).</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">∀F (Fa ↔ Fb) → a = b</span></td><td>Disputed. To write it you have to quantify over properties (∀F), and it is not a law of logic but a thesis about the world.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Leibniz defends the second with sufficient reason (section 3): if there were two perfectly identical things, God would have no reason to put one here and the other there. In his fourth letter to Samuel Clarke (June 1716) he tells how a gentleman of the court, in the gardens of Princess Sophia in Hanover, went all over the garden looking for two exactly identical leaves, and did not find them. The same, he adds, happens with two drops of water or of milk: looked at under the microscope, they can be told apart.</p>\n<p>The discussion is still open. The most famous counterexample is Max Black's (1952): imagine an empty universe with only two iron spheres of the same size, colour and temperature, at a certain distance from each other. What property does one have that the other lacks? Particle physics, with particles that seem indistinguishable, has reopened the debate.</p>\n<p><strong>Kant against Leibniz.</strong> Kant used precisely the drops of water against the principle. Two drops may be identical in everything their concept can say about them, and still they are two, because they occupy <strong>different places in space</strong> at the same time (<em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, ‘Amphiboly of the Concepts of Reflection’, A263-264/B319-320). Leibniz's mistake, according to Kant, was to treat appearances as if they were concepts of the understanding, forgetting that space is a form of sensibility (A271/B327). For concepts, the principle holds; for things in space, it does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> today the name ‘Leibniz's law’ is given to the <strong>indiscernibility of identicals</strong>, the one nobody disputes, and not to the identity of indiscernibles. They are converses: from ‘if they are identical, they are indiscernible’ it does not follow that ‘if they are indiscernible, they are identical’.</p>\n\n<h3>What research is debating today</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Boole before Boole?</strong> Lenzen maintains, from his studies of the 1980s up to <em>Calculus universalis</em> (2004), that the algebra of concepts is equivalent to Boolean algebra. He calls Leibniz ‘the most important logician between Aristotle and Frege’.</li>\n<li><strong>A complete reconstruction.</strong> In 2016 Marko Malink and Anubav Vasudevan published, in <em>The Review of Symbolic Logic</em>, the most rigorous reconstruction of the <em>General Inquiries</em>. They prove that the calculus is sound and complete with respect to a class of extended Boolean algebras, and that it reproduces the whole of propositional logic. On several points they correct earlier readings, including Lenzen's.</li>\n<li><strong>Reducing ‘if…, then’ to ‘S is P’.</strong> In 2019 the same authors studied how Leibniz tried to turn hypothetical judgements into categorical ones, treating a whole proposition as a term. ‘If it rains, the ground gets wet’ would then be read as ‘raining contains the ground getting wet’: the first proposition, treated as a term, contains the second, just as ‘human being’ contains ‘animal’.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Leibniz and Kant</h3>\n<p>What did reach Kant was the school logic of Leibniz's followers, such as Christian Wolff, and its central idea: in every true proposition, <strong>the predicate is contained in the subject</strong>. Kant uses it to define <strong>analytic judgements</strong>, those whose predicate is already thought, even if covertly, in the concept of the subject (A6/B10). But he breaks with Leibniz by asserting that not all truths are like this: there are <strong>synthetic</strong> judgements, and some of them are a priori.</p>\n<p>The reduction of hypotheticals to categoricals sheds light on this by contrast. Leibniz wanted to keep only one form of relation, the categorical. Kant keeps the three as distinct and irreducible, and from each one he derives a different category: <strong>substance</strong>, <strong>cause</strong> and <strong>community</strong>. If Leibniz were right, causality would be a disguised form of the subject-predicate relation, and Kant's answer to Hume would lose its basis.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Leibniz influenced modern logic’. He was a forerunner, not an influence. His calculi anticipated Boole, Frege, Lewis and Anderson, but they remained unpublished and modern logic was born without them. They were recognised later, when people already knew what to look for in his papers.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Two principles with a history: non-contradiction and sufficient reason</h2>\n<p>Leibniz places two principles at the basis of all reasoning (<em>Monadology</em>, 1714, §§ 31-32). On the <strong>principle of contradiction</strong> depend the <strong>truths of reason</strong>, such as those of mathematics, whose opposite is impossible. On the <strong>principle of sufficient reason</strong> depend the <strong>truths of fact</strong>, whose opposite is possible. Neither of them is his invention: they have more than two thousand years of history behind them, and Kant rethought them thoroughly.</p>\n\n<h3>The principle of non-contradiction</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before Aristotle.</strong> Parmenides had already posed the alternative between ‘is’ and ‘is not’, with no middle ground. Plato formulates something very close in the <em>Republic</em>: one and the same thing cannot do or undergo opposite things in the same respect, in relation to the same thing and at the same time (IV, 436b).</li>\n<li><strong>Aristotle</strong> turns it into ‘the firmest of all principles’: it is impossible for the same thing to belong and not to belong at the same time to the same thing and in the same respect (<em>Metaphysics</em>, IV, 3, 1005b19-20). In fact he states it in three ways, which Jan Łukasiewicz distinguished in 1910:\n<ul>\n<li>about things: nothing can be and not be the same;</li>\n<li>about propositions: two contradictory statements cannot both be true at the same time (1011b13-14);</li>\n<li>about what we think: nobody can really believe that something is and is not (1005b23-25).</li>\n</ul>\nIt cannot be proved, because every proof already presupposes it. But it can be defended by refutation: it is enough for whoever denies it to say something meaningful for them to be using it, and whoever refuses to say anything ‘is like a plant’. His targets are Heraclitus, with his unity of opposites, and sophists such as Protagoras.</li>\n<li><strong>Leibniz</strong> considers it a primitive and innate principle, which we all use even if we do not formulate it, and links it to identity: A is A, and cannot be non-A.</li>\n<li><strong>Kant</strong> formulates it like this: no thing has a predicate that contradicts it. He calls it ‘the universal and completely sufficient principle of all analytic knowledge’. For synthetic judgements, on the other hand, it is only a negative condition: the fact that they do not contradict themselves is not enough for them to be true (A150-151/B189-191). And he corrects the age-old formula, ‘it is impossible for something to be and not to be <em>at the same time</em>’. That ‘at the same time’ brings time into a purely logical principle: a person can be young and, later, not be young, without any contradiction (A152-153/B191-192).</li>\n<li><strong>Today</strong> it is written <span class=\"tv-f\">¬(p ∧ ¬p)</span>, and the truth table shows that it is true in every row: a tautology. Even so, some people dispute it. <strong>Dialetheists</strong>, such as Graham Priest, hold that some contradictions are true, for example the liar paradox (‘this sentence is false’). To reason with them, <strong>paraconsistent</strong> logics have been built, in which a contradiction does not allow you to deduce anything whatsoever.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Hegel denied the principle of non-contradiction’. This is a very widespread reading, but a debatable one. His dialectic moves forward precisely because a contradiction cannot be left as it is: it shows that something is wrong and forces us to overcome it.</p>\n\n<h3>The principle of sufficient reason</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Presocratics already used it.</strong> According to Aristotle, Anaximander explained that the Earth stays still at the centre because it has <strong>no reason</strong> to move in one direction rather than another (<em>On the Heavens</em>, II, 13). Parmenides argues that being cannot have come from nothing: why would it have arisen at one moment and not at another? (fr. 8). Leucippus, Democritus' teacher, says it openly: nothing happens at random, but everything for a reason and by necessity (fr. 2).</li>\n<li><strong>From Archimedes to Spinoza.</strong> Archimedes starts from the idea that a balance with equal weights at equal distances has no reason to tilt; Leibniz would quote the example. Plato writes in the <em>Timaeus</em> that everything that comes to be comes to be through some cause (28a). Avicenna requires a cause for something that might not exist to exist. In the Middle Ages the opposite idea prevailed, God's freedom to choose without a reason, and Abelard was condemned for holding that God had to create the best possible world. <strong>Spinoza</strong> takes it to the extreme: for each thing a cause or reason must be given why it exists or why it does not exist (<em>Ethics</em>, I, prop. 11).</li>\n<li><strong>Leibniz</strong> gives it a name (<em>principium rationis sufficientis</em>) and makes it one of the two pillars of his philosophy: there is no true fact without a sufficient reason for it being so and not otherwise. From it he derives the identity of indiscernibles (section 2) and the thesis that God chose the best of all possible worlds.</li>\n<li><strong>Hume</strong> calls it into question: we can imagine an effect without its cause without contradicting ourselves, so the principle is not a truth of reason (<em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em>, I, 3, 3). Even Euler complained about the ‘abuse’ made of it in proofs.</li>\n<li><strong>Kant</strong> dealt with it twice. As a young man, in the <em>New Elucidation</em> (1755), he examined it under the name of the principle of ‘determining reason’ and criticised the proof Wolff gave. In the <em>Critique</em> he saves it, but in a reduced form: it no longer holds for everything that exists, but only for appearances. ‘The principle of sufficient reason is the ground of possible experience’ (A200-201/B246), and it is the second analogy (section 11). If it is applied beyond experience, to the world as a whole, reason falls into antinomies (section 12).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> the famous <strong>Buridan's ass</strong>, which starves to death between two equal piles of hay because it has no reason to choose one, does not appear in the works of John Buridan (14th century). The situation is already in Aristotle (<em>On the Heavens</em>, II, 13), with a man as hungry as he is thirsty between food and drink, and Al-Ghazali (11th century) has a version with two dates. Buridan, in his commentary on that work of Aristotle's, speaks of a <strong>dog</strong> faced with two equal portions; the ass seems to be a caricature by his critics.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Judging: what the understanding does</h2>\n<p>Kant starts from a simple idea: everything the understanding does can be reduced to <strong>judging</strong>, that is, to uniting representations into one: ‘this body is heavy’, ‘if it rains, the ground gets wet’. That is why he says that the understanding can be thought of as a <strong>faculty of judging</strong> (<em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, A69/B94).</p>\n<p>If this is so, you only have to make a complete list of the possible forms of judging to have a complete list of the ways in which the understanding unites what we perceive. That list is the ‘guiding thread’ that leads to the categories.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The table of judgements</h2>\n<p>Kant takes the logic of his time, which came from Aristotle, and arranges the forms of judgement under four headings with three moments each (A70/B95):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Heading</th><th>Moments</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Quantity</strong>: how many are we talking about?</td><td>Universal · Particular · Singular</td><td>All metals are conductors · Some metals are magnetic · This metal is copper</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Quality</strong>: is something affirmed or denied?</td><td>Affirmative · Negative · Infinite</td><td>The soul is mortal · The soul is not mortal · The soul is non-mortal</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Relation</strong>: how are the parts joined?</td><td>Categorical · Hypothetical · Disjunctive</td><td>Water is liquid · If water is heated, it boils · Water is solid, liquid or gaseous</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Modality</strong>: with what force is it asserted?</td><td>Problematic · Assertoric · Apodictic</td><td>It may rain · It is raining · It must rain</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>There are three details that are often overlooked:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kant acknowledges that general logic could treat <strong>singular</strong> judgements as universal ones, and <strong>infinite</strong> judgements as affirmative ones (A71-72/B96-97). He separates them because here he is not interested only in form, but in the content they add to knowledge. ‘The soul is non-mortal’ affirms, but the only thing it says about the soul is that it lies in the unlimited remainder of what is not mortal. The example is his.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>disjunctive</strong> judgement, the alternatives exclude one another and together they cover everything (A73-74/B99). It is an exclusive and exhaustive ‘or’.</li>\n<li><strong>Modality</strong> adds nothing to the content of the judgement. It only says what value the assertion has for thought: possible, actual or necessary (A74/B99-100).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>6. Kant's table in the mould of present-day logic</h2>\n<p>Kant was wrong to think that logic was finished. A few decades after his death, George Boole (1847 and 1854) turned it into a calculus, and Gottlob Frege, in his <em>Begriffsschrift</em> (1879), created <strong>predicate logic</strong>. With it, reasoning with ‘all’, ‘some’, ‘if…, then’ and relations such as ‘x is greater than y’, which the syllogism could not handle, can be analysed with the same precision. In the 20th century, C. I. Lewis (1918) and Saul Kripke (1959-1963) also built a <strong>modal logic</strong> for ‘it is possible’ and ‘it is necessary’.</p>\n<p>The best way to understand Kant's table, and to see its limits, is to <strong>transpose</strong> it into that language. We use the symbols from Topic 4 in Year 1: ∀x, ‘for every x’; ∃x, ‘there is at least one x’; ¬, ‘not’; ∧, ‘and’; ∨, ‘or’; →, ‘if…, then’. To these we add □, ‘it is necessary that’, and ◇, ‘it is possible that’. A capital letter next to a lower-case one is a predicate applied to an individual: Ma, ‘a is mortal’.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Judgement</th><th>Example</th><th>In present-day logic</th><th>What is gained or lost</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Universal</td><td>All metals are conductors.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">∀x (Mx → Cx)</span></td><td>Precision is gained: ‘for anything, if it is a metal, it is a conductor’. It does not assert that metals exist.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Particular</td><td>Some metals are magnetic.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">∃x (Mx ∧ Gx)</span></td><td>It asserts that at least one exists, and nothing more: ‘some’ does not mean ‘not all’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Singular</td><td>This metal is copper.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">Ca</span></td><td><strong>Kant was right.</strong> Traditional logic treated the singular as a universal; present-day logic writes it differently, without a quantifier, with the name of an individual (a).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Affirmative</td><td>The soul is mortal.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">Ma</span></td><td>No change.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative</td><td>The soul is not mortal.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">¬Ma</span></td><td>No change.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infinite</td><td>The soul is non-mortal.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">¬Ma</span></td><td><strong>The distinction is lost.</strong> In predicate logic, ‘non-mortal’ and ‘is not mortal’ are written the same way. Only term logics, closer to Aristotle, distinguish negating a predicate from negating a sentence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Categorical</td><td>The soul is mortal · All metals are conductors.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">Ma</span> · <span class=\"tv-f\">∀x (Mx → Cx)</span></td><td>The form ‘S is P’ splits in two: predicating something of an individual, or relating two classes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypothetical</td><td>If there is perfect justice, the obstinately wicked person will be punished.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">p → q</span></td><td>As in Kant, it asserts neither p nor q: only their connection. But the present-day ‘→’ is weaker: it is true whenever p is false. For Kant, the connection is one of ground to consequence; the closest thing to it is □(p → q), Lewis's ‘strict’ implication, which Leibniz had already described (section 2).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disjunctive</td><td>The world exists through blind chance, or through inner necessity, or through an external cause.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">(p ∨ q ∨ r) ∧</span><br><span class=\"tv-f\">¬(p ∧ q) ∧ ¬(p ∧ r) ∧ ¬(q ∧ r)</span></td><td>The present-day ‘∨’ is <strong>inclusive</strong>. To say what Kant says, that one and only one of the alternatives is true, you have to add that no two of them hold at once.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Problematic</td><td>The world may have a beginning.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">◇p</span></td><td>It moves into modal logic, with its own rules: ◇p is equivalent to ¬□¬p (‘it is not necessary that not’).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Assertoric</td><td>The world has a beginning.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">p</span></td><td>No operator: it is the plain assertion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apodictic</td><td>Necessarily, every change has a cause.</td><td><span class=\"tv-f\">□∀x (Vx → ∃y Cyx)</span></td><td>(Vx: ‘x is a change’; Cyx: ‘y is a cause of x’.) <strong>The meaning changes.</strong> For Kant, modality adds nothing to the content of the judgement; in modal logic, □ is part of the content and changes its truth conditions.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Kant thought that every judgement has one form from each heading at the same time. The present-day mould lets us see this in a single example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>‘All bodies are heavy’ is universal, affirmative, categorical and assertoric: <span class=\"tv-f\">∀x (Kx → Px)</span>.</li>\n<li>‘If a body is released, it necessarily falls’ is universal, affirmative, hypothetical and apodictic: <span class=\"tv-f\">□∀x ((Kx ∧ Sx) → Cx)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Kant's causality is the → of logic’. No: ‘if p, then q’ is true in present-day logic even if p and q have nothing to do with each other, provided that p is false (‘if 2 + 2 = 5, the Moon is made of cheese’). For Kant, the category of cause comes from the hypothetical form of judgement, but that category only applies to experience through its temporal schema: <em>that which, once posited, is always followed by something else according to a rule</em> (section 10). Causality is necessary connection in time, not the connective.</p>\n<p>The transposition leaves a clear balance. Quantity survives, and present-day logic distinguishes it even better, because it treats the singular separately, as Kant wanted. Relation is shared out between predication and the connectives, and modality becomes a logic of its own. The infinite judgement disappears, merged with the negative. That is why few people today accept that the table of judgements is as complete and necessary as Kant believed. But his underlying question, whether our forms of thinking condition what we can know, remains open.</p>\n\n<h2>7. From judgements to categories</h2>\n<p>This is the decisive step, the so-called <strong>metaphysical deduction</strong>. Kant maintains that the operation that unites concepts in a judgement is the same one that unites the manifold that reaches us through the senses in the experience of an object:</p>\n<blockquote><p>‘The same function that gives unity to the different representations in a judgement also gives unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this function, expressed in general terms, is called a pure concept of the understanding.’ — Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, § 10, A79/B104-105 (own translation)</p></blockquote>\n<p>That is why each form of judgement has a corresponding pure concept. Kant calls them <strong>categories</strong> and says that he takes the name from Aristotle (A79-80/B105). But he does not share his method: Aristotle collected them ‘as they came up’, whereas Kant believes he has deduced them from a principle, the table of judgements.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Heading</th><th>Judgement</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\"><strong>Quantity</strong></td><td>Universal</td><td>Unity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Particular</td><td>Plurality</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Singular</td><td>Totality</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\"><strong>Quality</strong></td><td>Affirmative</td><td>Reality</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative</td><td>Negation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infinite</td><td>Limitation</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\"><strong>Relation</strong></td><td>Categorical</td><td>Substance and accident</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypothetical</td><td>Cause and effect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disjunctive</td><td>Community (reciprocal action)</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\"><strong>Modality</strong></td><td>Problematic</td><td>Possibility and impossibility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Assertoric</td><td>Existence and non-existence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apodictic</td><td>Necessity and contingency</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The clearest pairing is that of relation. Thinking ‘if A, then B’ (hypothetical judgement) is the logical form which, applied to what we perceive, gives <strong>causality</strong>: we do not see the cause, but we cannot think of an objective change without it. That is Kant's answer to Hume.</p>\n\n<h2>8. The twelve categories with examples (and three badly chosen examples)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Unity</td><td>Taking something as one</td><td>There is a book on the table.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plurality</td><td>Taking something as many</td><td>There are three books on the table.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Totality</td><td>Taking many as a whole</td><td>The whole collection is on the shelf.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reality</td><td>Something being given</td><td>The water is hot.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negation</td><td>Something not being given</td><td>The water is not hot.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Limitation</td><td>The real bounded by a negation</td><td>This wall is non-red: it falls within the rest of the colours.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Substance</td><td>What persists beneath what changes</td><td>The table is still the same table even if I paint it another colour.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>That which, once posited, is followed by something else according to a rule</td><td>I press the switch and the light comes on.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Community</td><td>Things that exist at the same time and act on one another</td><td>I can look first at the Moon and then at the Earth, or the other way round: they exist at the same time and influence each other.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Possibility</td><td>What fits the conditions of experience</td><td>It may rain tomorrow.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Existence</td><td>What is actually given in perception</td><td>It is raining today.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Necessity</td><td>What cannot fail to be given, according to the laws of experience</td><td>If water reaches 100 °C at sea level, it necessarily boils.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Trick cases.</strong> There are three examples that circulate widely and do not work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limitation is not ‘lukewarm’.</strong> It is not a midpoint between hot and cold, but the real thought of with a limit. That is why it corresponds to the infinite judgement: ‘the soul is non-mortal’ does not deny anything, but places the soul in a territory with no definite limits.</li>\n<li><strong>Necessity is not ‘the triangle has three sides’.</strong> That is a logical necessity, the necessity of a concept: denying it would be contradicting yourself. The category of necessity refers to the <strong>existence</strong> of something in experience: an effect is necessary when it follows from its cause according to the laws of nature (A227/B279-280).</li>\n<li><strong>Community is not a chain.</strong> ‘The Sun acts on the Earth, and the Earth on the Moon’ describes causes in a row, not a reciprocal relation. Kant's example is that of the Earth and the Moon: I can perceive them in whatever order I like because they exist at the same time (B257).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>9. Which judgement gives which category? An open problem</h2>\n<p>Look at quantity again. If we pair the two tables in order, the <strong>universal</strong> judgement (‘all S’) gets <strong>unity</strong>, and the <strong>singular</strong> judgement (‘this S’) gets <strong>totality</strong>. Shouldn't it be the other way round? A judgement about a single individual seems to call for unity, and one about all, for totality.</p>\n<p>Specialists do not agree. For some, such as Jonathan Bennett, it is a simple oversight by Kant, who elsewhere associates the singular judgement with unity. For others, such as Mirella Capozzi, the order is correct: the singular judgement takes an object as a closed whole, and that is the idea of totality. The universal judgement, by contrast, runs through the cases one by one.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> do not repeat as if it were obvious that ‘the singular judgement gives unity’. That is exactly what is under discussion.</p>\n\n<h2>10. How are the categories applied to what we see? The schematism</h2>\n<p>One problem remains. The categories are pure concepts; what we see, on the other hand, is sensible. How can something as abstract as ‘cause’ be applied to two concrete events? With empirical concepts there is no difficulty: the concept of a <em>plate</em> has something in common with the geometrical concept of a <em>circle</em> (A137/B176). With the categories, an intermediary is needed that is both intellectual and sensible. Kant finds it in <strong>time</strong>, which is the form of everything we perceive, and he calls this intermediary a <strong>schema</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote><p>‘The schemata are therefore nothing but a priori determinations of time according to rules.’ — Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, A145/B184 (own translation)</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Its schema</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Quantity</td><td><strong>Number</strong>: adding one to one, successively.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reality</td><td>What fills time, to a <strong>degree</strong> that can increase or decrease from zero.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Substance</td><td>The <strong>persistence</strong> of the real in time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>The real which, once posited, is <strong>always followed</strong> by something else.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Community</td><td>The <strong>simultaneity</strong> of the determinations of one thing with those of another, according to a general rule.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Possibility</td><td>Existing at <strong>some</strong> time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Existence</td><td>Existing at a <strong>determinate</strong> time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Necessity</td><td>Existing at <strong>all</strong> times.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Kant sums up the division like this: the categories of quantity, quality, relation and modality refer to the <strong>series</strong>, the <strong>content</strong>, the <strong>order</strong> and the <strong>sum total</strong> of time.</p>\n\n<h2>11. The laws of all experience: the principles</h2>\n<p>Applied by means of their schemata, the categories give rise to the <strong>principles of pure understanding</strong>: laws that any possible experience must obey, and which we therefore know a priori. They are the basis of the <strong>synthetic a priori judgements</strong> of physics.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Categories</th><th>Principles</th><th>What they say (own translation)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Quantity</td><td>Axioms of Intuition</td><td>‘All intuitions are extensive magnitudes’ (B202): everything we see occupies space and time, and can be measured.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quality</td><td>Anticipations of Perception</td><td>‘In all appearances the real, which is an object of sensation, has an intensive magnitude, that is, a degree’ (B207): a light can be more or less intense.</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Relation</td><td>First Analogy of Experience</td><td>‘In all change of appearances substance persists, and its quantity is neither increased nor diminished in nature’ (B224).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Second Analogy</td><td>‘All changes take place according to the law of the connection of cause and effect’ (B232).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Third Analogy</td><td>‘All substances, insofar as they can be perceived in space as simultaneous, are in universal reciprocal action’ (B256).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Modality</td><td>Postulates of Empirical Thinking</td><td>Whatever agrees with the formal conditions of experience is <strong>possible</strong>; whatever is connected with its material conditions (sensation) is <strong>actual</strong>; whatever has its connection with the actual determined by the general conditions of experience is <strong>necessary</strong> (A218/B265-266).</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Kant calls the first two <strong>mathematical</strong>, because they refer to what can be measured and their certainty is intuitive. He calls the last two <strong>dynamical</strong>, because they refer to the existence of things and their relations (B199-200). The second analogy is the direct answer to Hume: that every change has a cause is not learnt through custom. It is a condition for there being objective experience.</p>\n\n<h2>12. Beyond experience: the Ideas of reason</h2>\n<p>The understanding unites what we perceive; <strong>reason</strong> wants more: it seeks the condition of all conditions, something <strong>unconditioned</strong>. Kant derives its Ideas, once again, from logic: from the three forms of the syllogism, which correspond to the three judgements of relation (A323/B379).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Syllogism</th><th>Idea of reason</th><th>Illusion when we think we know it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Categorical</td><td>The <strong>soul</strong>: the ultimate subject</td><td>Paralogisms: false reasoning about the self</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypothetical</td><td>The <strong>world</strong>: the complete series of conditions</td><td>Antinomies: equally strong proofs for and against (does the world have a beginning? is there freedom?)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disjunctive</td><td><strong>God</strong>: the sum total of everything possible</td><td>Ideal of pure reason: the proofs of the existence of God</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Since there is no intuition of the unconditioned, the Ideas do not give <strong>knowledge</strong>: applying the categories to them as if they were objects produces the illusions of the <strong>transcendental dialectic</strong>. But they are not useless. They have a <strong>regulative use</strong>: they guide research, like a horizon that is never reached. And in the <em>Critique of Practical Reason</em> (1788), freedom, the immortality of the soul and the existence of God return as <strong>postulates</strong> of morality (you will see this in Topic 20).</p>\n\n<h2>13. A twentieth-century reading: Strawson’s reformulations</h2>\n<p>In 1966, the Oxford philosopher <strong>Peter F. Strawson</strong> (1919-2006) published <em>The Bounds of Sense</em> (published in Spanish as <em>Los límites del sentido</em>), a book that changed the way the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> was read in analytic philosophy. His proposal is to separate two Kants. One is the Kant of <strong>transcendental psychology</strong> (the faculties that ‘synthesise’ what is given) and of <strong>transcendental idealism</strong> (we know only phenomena, not things in themselves): Strawson rejects both. The other is an ‘austere’ Kant, who asks what any experience that we can conceive must contain. This is the Kant he wishes to save, and he reformulates him with arguments that do not need the rest of the system.</p>\n<h3>The principle of significance</h3>\n<p>Strawson sums up Kant’s critique of metaphysics in a single principle: a concept has a legitimate use only if it can be applied to experience. Hence the title of the book: there are <em>bounds of sense</em>, and traditional metaphysics crosses them when it uses the categories to speak of the soul, of the world as a totality or of God. This is the reading that fits with section 12: the Ideas of reason do not yield knowledge because there is no experience to which they could be applied.</p>\n<h3>The deduction reformulated: the argument from objectivity</h3>\n<p>What Strawson rescues from the transcendental deduction is an argument without faculties or synthesis, which can be reconstructed in five steps:</p>\n<ol><li>To be self-conscious requires being able to ascribe different experiences to oneself as one’s own.</li><li>To think of certain experiences as one’s own, one must be able to think of them <em>as experiences</em>, that is, as something distinct from that of which they are experiences.</li><li>This requires that experience allow us to distinguish between how things are and how they appear to me.</li><li>Only the experience of objects that do not depend on me provides this distinction.</li><li>Therefore the unity of consciousness requires experience of objects: an objective world.</li></ol>\n<p>It is a <strong>transcendental argument</strong>: it starts from something that no one can deny without contradicting themselves (that one has experiences and knows them to be one’s own) and concludes a necessary condition of that fact. Already in <em>Individuals</em> (1959) Strawson had used a similar argument against the sceptic: if we speak of things within a single system of space and time, we must be able to re-identify them, and this requires that they go on existing when no one perceives them. The sceptic, says Strawson, pretends to accept the conceptual scheme while silently rejecting one of its conditions.</p>\n<h3>What he does not save: the table of judgements and universal causality</h3>\n<p>Strawson does not accept that the categories are derived from the table of judgements (sections 7 and 9): he considers it a failed deduction, dependent on a logic that is no longer ours. Nor does he accept the proof, in the second analogy, that every change has a cause: he calls it ‘a <em>non sequitur</em> of numbing grossness’. What does follow, in his view, is something weaker: in order to distinguish an objective order of events from the order in which I perceive them, we need objects that persist and can be re-identified.</p>\n<h3>Stroud’s objection and Strawson’s retreat</h3>\n<p>In 1968, <strong>Barry Stroud</strong> objected to all transcendental arguments: at most they prove what we <em>have to believe</em> in order to think and speak as we do, not how things are. To make the leap from thought to the world, one would need verificationism or idealism, and with either of the two the argument would already be superfluous. Strawson ended up accepting the first part: in <em>Skepticism and Naturalism</em> (1985) he maintains, with Hume and with Wittgenstein, that sceptical doubt is not refuted but shown to be idle, because it cannot shift beliefs that form part of our nature. It is an open question: there is no agreement on what a transcendental argument can truly prove.</p>\n<p>For the Logic Corner: a transcendental argument has the form ‘<em>if</em> there is conscious experience, <em>then</em> there is an objective world; there is conscious experience; therefore…’. The form is a modus ponens (level 4 of the <a href=\"#logica/itinerario\">itinerary</a>). What is disputed is the truth of the first premise and what ‘then’ means in it: a condition of what we think or of what there is?</p>\n\n<h2>14. Something to think about</h2>\n<ol><li>Find your own example of a hypothetical judgement and explain which category it brings into play. Could you have experience of a world in which changes had no cause?</li><li>‘The number 7 is non-even’: what type of judgement is it, according to quality? What does it add compared with ‘7 is not even’?</li><li>Why does ‘the triangle has three sides’ not work as an example of the category of necessity?</li><li>Explain in your own words why Kant needs schemata. What would happen if the categories had nothing in common with the sensible?</li><li>Is saying that an Idea of reason is ‘regulative’ the same as saying that it is false?</li><li>Recast in the mould of contemporary logic: ‘Some philosophers are not empiricists’ and ‘If every change has a cause, the world did not begin by chance’. What type of judgement is each, according to Kant’s four headings?</li><li>Reconstruct in your own words Strawson’s argument from objectivity. Which step seems to you the most open to dispute? Does it answer Stroud’s objection?</li></ol>"}});
